      "text": "# Round 2 Independent Review: Discovery Manifest Roadmap\n\n## Posture\n\nI have read the artefact on its own merits and the two prior critiques. I agree with **approximately 70% of Reviewer A's structural concerns** and **30% of Reviewer B's process observations**, but both miss the single most dangerous sequencing dependency. Below I cite specific lines, identify new failure modes, and name the architectural call I would block outright.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Agreement with Prior Reviewers\n\n### Reviewer A: Trust Boundary Violation (Lines 389\u2013401, 724\u2013729)\n\n**Agree**. The scanner trusts frontmatter from any file under `.agent-src.uncondensed/` without verifying authorship. Reviewer A correctly identifies the supply-chain injection vector: a malicious `SKILL.md` claiming `trust.level: core` and `workspaces: [founder, finance]` would surface in every consumer's default install set.\n\nThe AI Council's mitigation (lines 724\u2013729: \"scanner only honours frontmatter on files under trusted roots\") is **insufficient** for the reason Reviewer A states: blocklists fail against symlinks, `.gitignore`d directories, or contributed PRs that land a skill in a trusted path before review.\n\n**However**, Reviewer A's proposed fix (a two-tier model with `pack-memberships.yml` as the authoritative source) is **operationally unworkable**. Here's why:\n\n- Every new skill would require touching **two files**: the skill's `SKILL.md` (declaring intent) and `pack-memberships.yml` (granting authority). This is exactly the maintenance burden line 25 says the roadmap was written to eliminate: *\"Es soll keine manuelle Package & Workspace Liste gepflegt werden.\"*\n- The mapping file would become a **merge bottleneck**. Two PRs adding skills to `engineering-base` would conflict on the same YAML block.\n\n**The correct fix** (which neither reviewer proposes):\n\n- The scanner honours frontmatter **only from files that passed code review in the main branch**. Concretely: `scripts/build_discovery_manifest.py` runs at release time, not on contributor PRs. A skill lands in the manifest only after it merges to `main`. This is already the design (line 598: \"release workflow runs `build_discovery_manifest.py` before `npm publish`\"), but the roadmap does not **explicitly state** that pre-merge CI never writes to the manifest. Add to Phase 5.1:\n  - [ ] Assert in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` that `dist/discovery/discovery-manifest.json` is **not present** in the working tree during PR checks. The manifest is a release artefact, not a PR artefact.\n\nThis closes the injection vector: a malicious skill can claim `trust.level: core` in its frontmatter, but that claim only enters the manifest **after** a maintainer merges the PR, at which point the claim is no longer malicious\u2014it's authorized.\n\n**Cite the missing line**: Nowhere in Phase 5 (lines 588\u2013605) does the roadmap say \"the manifest is release-time only, never written by PR CI.\" This is the gap.\n\n---\n\n### Reviewer A: Circular Dependency in Schema (Lines 208\u2013226, 300\u2013311)\n\n**Partially agree**. Reviewer A identifies that the JSON Schema defines `workspace.default_packs` and `pack.workspaces` as cross-references, but the vocabularies live in separate YAML files, creating **two sources of truth**. This is correct.\n\n**Disagree with the severity**. Reviewer A frames this as a schema bug (\"who wins when they disagree?\"), but the actual enforcement is clear from line 303: the **linter** (`lint_discovery_vocabulary.py`) asserts referential integrity, and the **schema** asserts structural shape. The schema is not *trying* to enforce referential integrity; that's the linter's job.\n\n**The real problem** is not circular dependency but **missing bidirectional enforcement**:\n\n- Phase 1.2 (lines 300\u2013311) checks that `workspaces.yml` default_packs **point to valid packs**.\n- It does **not** check that `packs.yml` workspaces **point to valid workspaces**.\n- It does **not** check that every pack referenced in `workspaces.yml` actually **lists that workspace** in its `workspaces:` array.\n\n**Example failure**:\n```yaml\n# workspaces.yml\n- id: engineering\n  default_packs: [php]\n\n# packs.yml\n- id: php\n  workspaces: [product]  # should be [engineering]\n```\n\nThe linter (as spec'd in lines 303\u2013305) would **pass** because `php` exists. The inconsistency is only detectable by cross-checking in both directions.\n\n**Fix**: Add to Phase 1.2's linter acceptance gate (line 321):\n- [ ] For every `workspaces.yml` entry `w` with `default_packs: [p1, p2]`, assert that every `p` in `packs.yml` has `w.id` in its `workspaces:` array. Bidirectional consistency.\n\n---\n\n### Reviewer B: Rollback Strategy Missing (General observation)\n\n**Agree**. Reviewer B correctly notes the proposal \"lacks explicit rollback strategies.\" Reviewer A frames this as \"missing kill-switch criteria\" (their Section 2). Both are right.\n\n**The specific failure mode**:\n\n- Release `v1.2.3` ships with a scanner bug. Example: every skill with `domain: engineering` gets mapped to `packs: [finance-basic]` due to a typo in the mapping logic (lines 544\u2013547 in Phase 4.1).\n- A user installs `v1.2.3`, runs the wizard, selects \"Finance,\" and gets engineering skills. They report \"broken package.\"\n- You publish `v1.2.4` with a fixed scanner. The manifest in `v1.2.4` is correct.\n\n**Question**: Does the user's runtime cache the old manifest from `v1.2.3`?\n\n- **If yes** (manifest is cached in `~/.agent-config/`), publishing `v1.2.4` does not fix the user's installation. They are pinned to the broken manifest until they manually delete the cache. The roadmap does not document this.\n- **If no** (manifest is re-read from `node_modules/` on every CLI invocation, per line 468), then the manifest is **mutable state** that changes under the user's feet when they `npm install`. This breaks the determinism guarantee from Phase 2.3 (lines 446\u2013450).\n\n**The roadmap does not answer this question**. Phase 3.1 (lines 468\u2013478) says `loadManifest()` \"locates and parses the manifest,\" but does not specify the lookup order. Is it:\n1. `~/.agent-config/manifest-cache/<package-version>/discovery-manifest.json` (versioned cache)?\n2. `node_modules/@event4u/agent-config/dist/discovery/discovery-manifest.json` (no cache, always fresh)?\n3. Something else?\n\n**Fix**: Add to Phase 3.1:\n- [ ] `loadManifest()` reads from `node_modules/@event4u/agent-config/dist/discovery/discovery-manifest.json` **only**. No cache. If the manifest is missing, throw `ManifestNotFoundError`. Document this in a \"Manifest Lifecycle\" section in `docs/architecture/discovery-manifest-lifecycle.md`.\n\n**Kill-switch**: Reviewer A proposes a `discovery-manifest.fallback.json` shipped alongside the primary manifest. This is **overkill for v1**. The kill-switch is simpler: if the manifest fails schema validation (Phase 2.2, lines 430\u2013437), the CLI aborts with \"corrupted manifest detected; reinstall the package.\" The user's recourse is `npm install --force`. This is sufficient for a v1 delivery where the manifest is **not yet mission-critical** (the CLI can still function without it; it just can't run the wizard). Add to Phase 2.2:\n- [ ] If `lint_discovery_manifest.py` exits non-zero, the CLI must **refuse to start** (not just log a warning). A corrupted manifest is a contract violation.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Disagreement with Prior Reviewers\n\n### Reviewer A: \"Phase 4 is Unrollbackable\" (Lines 538\u2013584)\n\n**Disagree with the framing, agree with the underlying risk**. Reviewer A argues that Phase 4 (annotating every artefact) is a \"deployment atomic-bomb\" because it touches 200+ files in one PR. Their proposed fix is to split it into \"one PR per workspace.\"\n\n**This is the wrong granularity**. Here's why:\n\n- A workspace like `engineering` spans **multiple packs** (`engineering-base`, `php`, `laravel`, `javascript`, `typescript`, `react`, `nextjs`, `python`). Annotating \"the engineering workspace\" means touching artefacts across 8 packs.\n- Two contributors could simultaneously author PRs for different workspaces (`engineering` and `finance`), and those PRs would **conflict on shared packs** (e.g., `git` belongs to both).\n\n**The correct granularity is pack-by-pack, not workspace-by-workspace**. Packs are cohesive (all artefacts in `laravel` live under one directory), whereas workspaces are cross-cutting. Revise Phase 4's acceptance gate (lines 583\u2013584):\n\n- [ ] **Old**: \"One mass-edit commit per artefact category (skills / rules / commands / templates).\"\n- [ ] **New**: \"One PR per pack (`engineering-base`, `php`, `laravel`, \u2026). Each PR touches only the artefacts in that pack's directory tree. The final PR (`meta` pack) updates `unassigned-artefacts.yml`.\"\n\nThis also fixes Reviewer A's rollback concern: if the `laravel` pack is mis-annotated, you roll back **one pack** by reverting one PR, not by re-annotating 200 files.\n\n**However**, Reviewer A is correct that the roadmap omits a **manifest diff report**. Add to Phase 4.5 (after line 584):\n- [ ] Commit `agents/evidence/notes/discovery-manifest-diff-phase4.md` listing every artefact whose `packs:` / `workspaces:` assignment changed from the Phase 3 baseline (where every artefact was in `unassigned[]`). This is the audit trail.\n\n---\n\n### Reviewer A: \"The Manifest Should Be User-Editable\" (Section 5)\n\n**Strongly disagree**. Reviewer A's core architectural objection is that the manifest is a \"static artefact shipped in the tarball\" (lines 49\u201352), and proposes instead:\n\n> \"The workspace/pack bindings live in a **user-editable config file** (`~/.agent-config/workspaces.yml`) that references artefact IDs from the base manifest.\"\n\n**This is the wrong layer for two reasons**:\n\n1. **It conflates distribution with customization**. The manifest is a **distribution artefact** (what the package ships), not a **runtime config** (what the user runs). The user's customization layer is **already defined** in ADR-010 (lines 16\u201319): profiles, presets, and cost_profile. The user selects a preset (`laravel-startup`); the preset declares which packs to load. The user does **not** edit `workspaces.yml` to define a custom workspace\u2014they create a custom **preset** that pulls from existing packs.\n\n2. **It inverts the dependency**. Reviewer A's design makes the **base manifest** declare \"artefacts and their intrinsic properties\" (type, path, trust), and the **user bindings** declare workspace/pack membership. But pack membership **is intrinsic** to an artefact. A Laravel skill is a Laravel skill because its author declared it so in `packs: [laravel]`, not because the user decided to file it under \"Engineering.\" The user's only job is to **select which packs to activate**, not to redefine which artefacts belong to which packs.\n\n**Reviewer A's forcing function** (\"the user wants to define a custom workspace that pulls skills from multiple packs\") is **already possible** in the current design:\n\n- The user creates a custom **preset** (ADR-010, lines 16\u201319) that lists `packs: [laravel, react, gtm-sales]`.\n- The preset is a **user-editable file** under `~/.agent-config/presets/my-company.yml`.\n- The manifest is **not touched**.\n\n**Cite the line that proves this**: Lines 16\u201319 (ADR-010 reference) already establish the four-axes model. The roadmap does **not need** to add a fifth axis (user-defined workspaces) because that's what presets are for.\n\n**What evidence would change my mind**: If the roadmap removed the preset system and said \"workspaces are the only customization layer,\" then Reviewer A's objection would be valid. But ADR-010 is a prerequisite (line 16), so the objection is moot.\n\n---\n\n### Reviewer B: \"Phase 0 Should Include Prototypes\" (Final section)\n\n**Disagree**. Reviewer B suggests Phase 0 should include \"potential prototype implementations of key components\" to provide \"empirical feedback\" before later phases. **This is scope creep**.\n\nPhase 0 is explicitly **\"no code is written\"** (line 160). Its purpose is to lock the **contract** (the shape of frontmatter, the schema of the manifest) so that Phases 1\u20137 build against a stable foundation. A prototype in Phase 0 would:\n\n- Invalidate the contract the moment the prototype revealed a design flaw.\n- Create throwaway code that would need to be rewritten in Phase 2 (the real scanner).\n\n**The correct sequencing** is:\n\n- Phase 0: Agree on the contract (frontmatter keys, vocabulary values, schema shape).\n- Phase 1: Write the linters that enforce the contract (no scanner yet).\n- Phase 2: Write the scanner that emits the contract (first code that produces a manifest).\n- Phase 3: Write the consumers that read the contract (CLI, server route).\n\nA prototype in Phase 0 would collapse Phases 0\u20132 into one mega-phase, exactly the kind of \"too much upfront design\" that roadmaps are meant to avoid.\n\n**Cite the line**: Line 160 (\"No code is written in Phase 0\") is **intentional**, not an oversight. If Reviewer B's concern is \"the contract might be wrong,\" the fix is better stakeholder review in Phase 0, not adding code.\n\n---\n\n## 3. New Points Not Raised in Round 1\n\n### Critical: Phase 5 Assumes Phase 3 Deployed (Lines 588\u2013605)\n\n**This is the single most dangerous sequencing dependency, and neither reviewer caught it**. Here's the failure mode:\n\n- **Phase 3** (lines 460\u2013519) writes the TS consumers (`agent-config workspaces ls`, `agent-config packs ls`, the Fastify route `/api/v1/discovery/manifest`). Phase 3's acceptance gate (lines 514\u2013519) asserts these commands **work when run locally** against a freshly-built manifest.\n- **Phase 5** (lines 588\u2013605) wires the manifest generation into the **release workflow** (`.github/workflows/release.yml`). It adds a step \"Build discovery manifest\" that runs `python3 scripts/build_discovery_manifest.py --write --strict` **before `npm publish`**.\n\n**The problem**: Phase 5 ships the manifest in the npm tarball (line 595: `package.json \"files\"` includes `dist/discovery/`), but Phase 5 does **not** ship the TS consumers. The consumers are part of the **CLI binary** (`dist/cli/agent-config.js`), which is built by a **different roadmap** (`typescript-cli-and-local-gui-foundation`, line 13, prerequisite).\n\n**Scenario**:\n\n1. Phase 5 merges. The release workflow runs. `npm publish` ships `v1.2.3`.\n2. A user installs `v1.2.3` and runs `agent-config workspaces ls`.\n3. **The command does not exist** because the TS-foundation roadmap has not merged yet. The user sees \"command not found.\"\n\n**Why neither reviewer caught this**: Both focused on the **scanner** (Phase 2) and the **manifest** (Phase 5), but neither checked whether the **consumers** (Phase 3) would be **live in the same release** as the manifest. The roadmap treats Phase 3 as a local dev task (\"add these commands to the CLI\"), but does not verify that the CLI is **part of the tarball**.\n\n**The fix**: Add to Phase 3's prerequisites (line 460, new bullet):\n\n- [ ] Confirm `dist/cli/agent-config.js` is included in `package.json \"files\"` and that the release workflow runs `npm run build:cli` (from the TS-foundation roadmap) **before** `npm run build:discovery`.\n\n**If the TS-foundation roadmap has not merged**, Phase 3 is **blocked**. The roadmap must say this explicitly. Revise line 13:\n\n- [ ] **Old**: \"Roadmap `typescript-cli-and-local-gui-foundation.md` is **status: completed** and merged.\"\n- [ ] **New**: \"Roadmap `typescript-cli-and-local-gui-foundation.md` is **status: completed**, merged, **and shipped in a published npm version**. Run `npm info @event4u/agent-config` and assert `dist/cli/agent-config.js` is present in the tarball file list.\"\n\nThis is the **single blocking dependency** the roadmap glosses over.\n\n---\n\n### Missing: What Happens When the Vocabulary Changes? (Lines 264\u2013290, Phase 1.1)\n\nThe roadmap writes the closed vocabularies (`workspaces.yml`, `packs.yml`) in Phase 1.1 (lines 264\u2013290), and ADR-013 (line 206) says \"new entries require an ADR amendment.\" **But the roadmap does not specify what happens to existing manifests when the vocabulary grows**.\n\n**Scenario**:\n\n- Release `v1.2.3` ships with `workspaces: [engineering, product, finance, founder, gtm, ops, small-business, construction, agent-config-maintainer]` (9 workspaces).\n- Six months later, a contributor proposes adding `workspaces: [legal]` (for legal-ops skills). An ADR amendment is written and approved.\n- Release `v1.3.0` ships with 10 workspaces.\n\n**Question**: Does a skill annotated in `v1.2.3` with `workspaces: [legal]` (before `legal` was in the vocabulary) cause the scanner to fail in `v1.2.3`'s release workflow?\n\n- **Yes**: The scanner (Phase 2.1, lines 396\u2013401) validates \"every value against `config/discovery/{workspaces,packs}.yml`\" and pushes unknown values to `unassigned[]` (line 400). If a skill claims `workspaces: [legal]` before `legal` is in the vocabulary, the skill is **unassigned** (warning in non-strict mode, failure in strict mode after Phase 4.4).\n- **No**: The contributor's PR must **first** amend `workspaces.yml`, **then** annotate the skill. This means the vocabulary amendment and the skill annotation land in the **same PR**, which is a large change surface.\n\n**The roadmap does not specify which**. Add to ADR-013 (Phase 0.2, line 206):\n\n- [ ] \"Vocabulary amendments follow this sequencing: (1) Update `config/discovery/workspaces.yml` or `packs.yml`. (2) Merge and release. (3) Annotate artefacts in a subsequent PR. A PR that adds a vocabulary term and annotates artefacts using that term in the same commit is **prohibited**\u2014the vocabulary must exist in a released version before artefacts reference it.\"\n\nThis prevents the \"chicken-and-egg\" problem and makes vocabulary changes auditable (one PR per vocabulary term, separate from the PRs that use it).\n\n---\n\n### Missing: The Wizard's Fail-Fast Behaviour (Line 91)\n\nLine 91 (Phase 0 prerequisites) says:\n\n> \"The setup wizard (`unified-setup-and-settings-gui`) reads the manifest via `/api/v1/discovery/manifest` and **fails to start** if the file is missing \u2014 no silent fallback to hard-coded data.\"\n\n**This is a UX failure**. If the user installs `@event4u/agent-config` and the tarball is corrupted (the manifest is missing due to a release-workflow bug), the wizard crashes on startup with no recourse. The user cannot proceed.\n\n**Compare** Phase 3.2 (lines 489\u2013493), which says the Fastify route returns `503 + { error: 'manifest_not_found' }` on a missing manifest. This is **inconsistent**: the HTTP layer says \"service unavailable\" (a recoverable error), but the wizard says \"fail to start\" (a crash).\n\n**The correct behaviour**:\n\n- The wizard **tries** to fetch the manifest.\n- If the fetch returns 503, the wizard displays a **fallback screen**: \"Manifest not available. You can install manually by selecting individual skills, or reinstall the package.\"\n- The wizard does **not crash**.\n\n**Why this matters**: A missing manifest should **not** brick the installation. The manifest is a convenience layer (it drives the workspace/pack UI), not a hard dependency (the user can still manually select skills by reading `SKILL.md` files directly).\n\n**Fix**: Revise line 91:\n\n- [ ] **Old**: \"The setup wizard \u2026 **fails to start** if the file is missing.\"\n- [ ] **New**: \"The setup wizard \u2026 **displays a fallback UI** if the manifest is missing or returns 503. The fallback allows manual skill selection but disables workspace/pack toggles.\"\n\n---\n\n## 4. The Single Architectural Call I Would Push Back On\n\n### The Scanner Runs at Release Time, Not at PR Time (Lines 598\u2013605, Phase 5.1)\n\n**This is the wrong build stage**. The roadmap places the scanner in the **release workflow** (line 598: \"`.github/workflows/release.yml` runs `build_discovery_manifest.py` before `npm publish`\"). The stated rationale (implied, not explicit) is that the manifest is a release artefact, so it should be built at release time.\n\n**This is dangerous for two reasons**:\n\n1. **The scanner can fail in the release workflow**. Example: A contributor merges a PR that adds a skill with invalid frontmatter (`workspaces: [unknown]`). The PR's CI does not run the scanner (because the manifest is a release artefact, not a PR artefact). The release workflow runs, the scanner fails, `npm publish` aborts. **The release is blocked**. The fix requires a hotfix PR, which delays the release by hours or days.\n\n2. **The manifest is not reviewable before merge**. If the scanner runs only at release time, contributors and reviewers never see the manifest diff. A skill's `workspaces:` annotation could be wrong, and no one notices until the release ships.\n\n**The correct design**: The scanner runs **in PR CI**, but the manifest is **not committed**. Concretely:\n\n- Add a CI step (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`):\n  ```yaml\n  - name: Validate discovery annotations\n    run: |\n      python3 scripts/build_discovery_manifest.py --write --strict\n      python3 scripts/lint_discovery_manifest.py --quiet\n      git diff --exit-code dist/discovery/  # assert no changes if re-run\n  ```\n- The PR CI builds the manifest into `dist/discovery/` (which is gitignored), validates it, and asserts determinism. If the scanner fails, **the PR is blocked**.\n- The release workflow runs the scanner again (for determinism), but this is a **no-op** if the PR already passed.\n\n**Why this is better**:\n\n- **Fail fast**: Invalid frontmatter is caught at PR time, not release time.\n- **Reviewable**: The PR's CI logs show the manifest diff (via a GitHub Actions artefact upload). Reviewers can verify the skill is in the correct pack.\n- **No release blocking**: The release workflow cannot fail due to scanner issues because the PR already validated the input.\n\n**What evidence would change my mind**:\n\n- A forcing function: \"The scanner must never run on contributor machines or in PR CI because it is a privileged operation (e.g., it signs the manifest with a release key).\" This would justify release-time-only scanning. But the roadmap does **not** propose signing in Phase 5 (line 600's `check_release_includes_discovery.py` only checks for existence, not signature). So the scanner is not privileged.\n\n**Cite the line**: Line 598 (\"The release workflow runs `build_discovery_manifest.py` before `npm publish`\") is the decision point. This must move to PR CI with the release workflow as a verification step.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Summary & Recommendation\n\n### Block outright:\n- **Phase 5.1 (release-time scanning)**. Move the scanner to PR CI per Section 4. The release workflow becomes a verification gate, not the primary build.\n\n### Require before Phase 1 starts:\n- **Trust-root assertion** (Section 1, agreement with Reviewer A): Add to Phase 5.1 a CI assertion that `dist/discovery/` is not present during PR checks.\n- **Bidirectional vocabulary lint** (Section 1, disagreement with Reviewer A): Add to Phase 1.2's linter the cross-check that `workspaces.yml` and `packs.yml` are mutually consistent.\n- **Manifest lookup order** (Section 1, agreement with Reviewer B): Document in Phase 3.1 that `loadManifest()` reads from `node_modules/` only, no cache.\n\n### Require before Phase 4 starts:\n- **Pack-by-pack granularity** (Section 2): Revise Phase 4's acceptance gate to \"one PR per pack.\"\n- **TS-foundation shipped** (Section 3, new point): Add to Phase 3's prerequisites a check that `dist/cli/agent-config.js` is in the published tarball.\n\n### Require before status \u2192 proposed:\n- **Vocabulary amendment sequencing** (Section 3, new point): Add to ADR-013 the rule that vocabulary changes and artefact annotations happen in separate PRs.\n- **Wizard fallback UI** (Section 3, new point): Revise line 91 to allow graceful degradation on missing manifest.\n\n### As written, this roadmap is **75% deliverable**. The core primitive (frontmatter \u2192 scanner \u2192 manifest \u2192 consumers) is sound. The trust model (Section 1) and the build stage (Section 4) are not. Fix those two, and I would greenlight Phases 0\u20134. Phases 5\u20137 are **blocked until the TS-foundation roadmap ships**.",
